5. Critical data controls
These controls should be incorporated from the beginning, rather than addressed after extraction.
Transaction deduplication: Establish a unique transaction identifier based on account, date, amount, direction, transaction reference and other distinguishing attributes. A single transfer appearing in a statement, Excel export and FedWire confirmation should count as one transaction with multiple source references.
Source provenance: Every transaction should retain the source document filename, Bates number, document type, and where feasible, page or record reference. Supplemental evidence for a beneficiary or originating bank should be separately cited.
Financial reconciliation: Compare extracted credits and debits against statement activity and opening/closing balances. Record unresolved differences instead of silently forcing balances to reconcile.
Inter-account movements: Tag likely internal transfers so that tracing can show the movement of funds without presenting the same money as new external inflows each time it transfers between accounts.
Evidence boundaries: Do not incorporate restricted materials from earlier litigation. Public records or records properly produced in the current litigation may be considered subject to legal-team confirmation.
Data limitations: Flag periods for which bank statements are unavailable because of retention policies. Missing statements should not be interpreted as evidence that an account was inactive.
6. Recommended deliverable structure
I recommend using a common Excel schema for each account so the files can be combined without additional transformation.
Deliverable	Contents
Account-level workbooks	Complete normalized transactions for each of the eight priority accounts
Master transaction workbook	Combined records across the eight accounts with account and ownership fields
FedWire master workbook	All available FedWire transactions, including those from non-priority accounts
Cash activity report	ATM withdrawals, teller cash transactions and checks payable to cash
Reconciliation summary	Opening/closing balances, credits, debits, duplicate counts and exceptions
Source reference index	Document-to-transaction mapping with Bates references
Findings and exceptions log	Significant movement patterns, gaps, discrepancies and unresolved questions
A particularly valuable additional field is Related Transaction ID, which will help establish a connection between an outgoing transfer from one account and an incoming transfer to another.
